Obstructive Sleep Apnoea (OSA)
Respiratory System
Loud snoring/apneas, Excessive daytime sleepiness, High AHI on polysomnography

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-032
Reason for Visit: Extreme partner distress regarding witnessed apneas and severe morning headaches in the patient, requiring urgent assessment and sleep study referral.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and highly distressed partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a partner who had stayed awake observing the patient stop breathing for prolonged periods, followed by gasping and choking. The patient complained of a severe morning headache and near-syncope upon waking. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute arrhythmias or severe nocturnal hypoxemia prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My wife says I stopped breathing for a full minute last night and turned blue, and I woke up feeling like I've been hit by a truck with a splitting headache.
History of Present Illness: The partner reports a long history of loud, disruptive snoring that is now repeatedly interrupted by sudden pauses in breathing (witnessed apneic episodes) followed by gasping and choking. The patient reports excessive daytime sleepiness, chronically non-restorative sleep, and frequent dull morning headaches. They complain of a severely dry mouth and sore throat upon awakening. Over the last six months, the patient has noted increased irritability, mood swings, impaired concentration at work, and decreased libido.
Functional Status: Independent, but struggling to maintain alertness during the day (e.g., falling asleep at traffic lights).
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk for motor vehicle accidents due to micro-sleeps.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 150/92 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 16 breaths/min | SpO2: 97% on room air | Wt: 110 Kg
General: High body mass index (BMI > 35 kg/m2), thick neck circumference (>43cm), appears fatigued, yawning frequently during the consultation.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals a crowded airway visualized as a high Mallampati score (Class IV), with enlarged tonsils and a long, thick soft palate. Nasal exam shows mildly deviated septum. Chest auscultation is clear. Cardiovascular exam reveals elevated resting blood pressure, likely secondary to chronic nocturnal sympathetic surges.
Environmental Observations: Partner has moved to a separate bedroom due to the severe snoring volume.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications; using over-the-counter caffeine pills heavily.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Obstructive Sleep Apnoea (OSA) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Central Sleep Apnea - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of loud snoring and gasping (indicating physical obstruction and respiratory effort), thick neck circumference, and high BMI, which overwhelmingly favor an obstructive etiology.

Primary Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While it can cause fatigue and weight gain, the witnessed clear apneic episodes and anatomical airway crowding make OSA the primary active diagnosis.

Narcolepsy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of cataplexy, sleep paralysis, or hypnagogic hallucinations, and the clear presence of disruptive snoring and witnessed respiratory pauses.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Driving risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, highly symptomatic Obstructive Sleep Apnoea (OSA). The clinical tetrad of loud snoring, witnessed apneas, excessive daytime sleepiness, and anatomical risk factors (thick neck, high Mallampati score) is overt. The partner's observation of prolonged apneas and the patient's severe morning headache indicate significant nocturnal hypoxemia and hypercapnia. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient undiagnosed and at extreme risk of a motor vehicle accident due to daytime somnolence, as well as unmanaged severe hypertension. An urgent after-hours home visit was required to perform an airway assessment, formally mandate driving restrictions, and initiate an urgent pathway for in-lab polysomnography.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiated an urgent referral to a Sleep Physician for an in-lab polysomnography (to confirm AHI >5 and titrate CPAP). Provided strict medical advice regarding driving cessation.
Positive Behavioral Change: Sleep strictly on your side with a firm pillow wedged behind your back; maintaining this lateral position physically prevents the heavy tissues of your throat and tongue from collapsing backward under gravity, directly reducing the number of times you stop breathing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Mandated temporary cessation of driving until cleared by the sleep specialist.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to check blood pressure and ensure the sleep study referral has been actioned.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation (call 000) if you wake up with severe central chest pain, if your heart is racing uncontrollably and irregularly, or if you develop sudden severe shortness of breath.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A formal diagnosis and exact severity grading (AHI) require an overnight sleep study; CPAP therapy cannot be prescribed until this is completed.
Clinical Course: The extreme fatigue and morning headaches will resolve almost completely once a CPAP machine physically splints your airway open during sleep.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must not drive any motor vehicle or operate heavy machinery until a specialist confirms your sleep apnea is effectively treated.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: When your muscles relax during sleep, the heavy tissues in your thick neck collapse and completely block your windpipe; your brain has to constantly wake you up in a panic to breathe, preventing you from ever getting deep, restful sleep.
Actionable Steps: Completely avoid alcohol for at least 4 hours before bed; alcohol acts as a muscle relaxant, making your throat muscles significantly floppier and directly worsening the airway collapse and choking.

